      "text": "We need to find the number of different ways to arrange the four team members as the first, second, third, and fourth runners in a relay race. \n\nSince Jordan is fixed in the fourth position, we only need to arrange the three remaining team members in the other three positions. The number of ways to arrange 3 items in 3 positions is given by the factorial of 3, denoted as \\(3!\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the factorial of 3:\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different orders in which Jordan's team members can run, given that Jordan is fixed in the fourth position, is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
