    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of different orders in which Jordan's three friends can run the first three laps, we need to consider the total number of permutations of their three friends. Since Jordan is the only one who runs the fourth lap, the problem reduces to finding the number of permutations of three individuals.\n\nThe number of permutations of three distinct objects is given by the factorial of 3, denoted as \\(3!\\). The factorial of a number \\(n\\) is the product of all positive integers up to \\(n\\). Therefore, we calculate \\(3!\\) as follows:\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\nSo, there are 6 different ways to order Jordan's three friends for the first three laps. The specific order in which they run the laps is not specified, but rather the relative order of the three friends is determined by the permutation.\n\nTo illustrate, the possible orders are:\n1. Friend 1, Friend 2, Friend 3\n2. Friend 1, Friend 3, Friend 2\n3. Friend 2, Friend 1, Friend 3\n4. Friend 2, Friend 3, Friend 1\n5. Friend 3, Friend 1, Friend 2\n6. Friend 3, Friend 2, Friend 1\n\nThus, the number of different orders in which the four team members can run, with Jordan running the fourth lap, is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
